window.docContents[144].resolve({"/The-standard-library/#Core-C______--The-standard-library":{"contents":"The headers of Core C++ declare the entities of the library without a body, the class templates std::vector and std::function and the function assert. Each declared name has a module, which holds relations and functions. The relations, in CoreCpp/Semantics/Library.lean, specify the entity in the forms of the judgments of the language. The rules of the language take them as premises. The functions, in CoreCpp/Std/, implement them, and the type checker and the evaluator call a function where a rule has its relation as a premise.\n\nThe type checker and the evaluator know no module by name. They find the module of a declared name. A use of an undeclared entity is a type error, so a program uses the library only through its #include.\n\nNothing of this is built into the syntax. The grammar has no production and the lexer no reserved word for the library. The declaration namespace std opens an ordinary namespace, and std::vector is an ordinary qualified name. The case of the name tells a class of the library, lowercase, from a class of the program, uppercase.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Core C++","header":"8. The standard library","id":"/The-standard-library/#Core-C______--The-standard-library"}});